[SourceVersionDate "1997.08.31"]
[SourceQuality "1"]

{Two years ago, I tied first in the Challengers' Tournament at Hastings, missing qualifying for the Premier on a narrow tie-breaking margin only This time I finished bottom. The field was almost exactly as strong. I played, overall, as well. Does that sound strange? It is true! Just about once in each game I played one bad move. The perils of inconsistency in chess have rarely been so comically exemplified. Look at these two examples [the other is Basman-Wood from the same event - game score available at BritBase - JS] which I could match with others: I played} 1... Kd4 $4 {- don't ask me why.} ({The obvious move [here] is} 1... a5 {; the pawn goes to a3 without hindrance, and can easily be queened without allowing White to sacrifice his bishop for it. A plausible continuation is} 2. Kg3 a4 3. Kf2 a3 4. Bb1 Kd4 5. Ke2 Kc3 6. Kd1 Kb2 7. Bh7 a2 {, etc. How could White improve on this? [With the benefit of a tablebase, we now know that White is lost after 1...a5, or indeed 1...Kb5 or 1...Nc6 - but it is a draw after 1...Kd5 - JS]}) 2. Bg6 $1 a5 3. Be8 {Now the pawn cannot go to a4 without a time-wasting struggle. White's king was able to get across the board just in time to draw.} 1/2-1/2

